// Exercise catalogue — ported from the Claude Design prototype (EX + DETAILS),
// then extended with the lower-body catalogue (quadriceps / glutes focus).
//
// `icon` names are Phosphor icon slugs (without the `ph-` prefix), consumed by
// <Icon /> — only slugs already registered in components/ui/Icon.jsx.
// `mat` values come from a closed vocabulary (Sans matériel · Haltères ·
// Élastique · Salle · Maison): the library filter matches them literally.
// `primaire` stays a single string; movements with several primary muscles list
// them joined with ' · '.
//
// Every entry also carries the two fields the "Muscle ciblé" block of the
// exercise sheet is built from:
//   sollicitation  how `primaire` is actually loaded by this movement — the
//                  contraction regime (excentrique / concentrique / isométrique)
//                  and where in the range the tension peaks. Describes the
//                  mechanism, not the technique: the how-to lives in
//                  depart/mouvement/respiration.
//   surcharge      what to watch so that muscle (or the joint and tendon it
//                  pulls on) is not overworked — the failure mode specific to
//                  this movement plus a concrete guardrail. Distinct from
//                  `conseils`, which are execution cues for a single rep.
// Both are prose aimed at the user, and stay training advice: nothing here
// diagnoses or treats, and the app shows a medical disclaimer on first run.
//
// Two further fields were added for the lower-body catalogue:
//   pattern    movement pattern the exercise belongs to, one of PATTERNS.
//              Only set on the lower-body entries; the original upper-body and
//              core exercises are untagged.
//   optionnel  true for an exercise that needs a specialised piece of kit (a GHR
//              bench, an ab wheel, a wrist roller…) or a skill/strength ceiling well
//              above the rest of its family (toes-to-bar past hanging leg raise,
//              pistol squat past a split squat) — the ones a beginner browsing the
//              default library doesn't need surfaced. It started scoped to calves
//              and a handful of advanced lower-body lifts when the catalogue was
//              thighs/glutes-only; now that the catalogue spans the whole body, the
//              same bar applies everywhere, so a niche or high-ceiling upper-body
//              or core entry can carry it too, `pattern` or not. `niveau: 'Avancé'`
//              alone is not the bar — most Avancé entries (tractions, hanging leg
//              raise) stay in the default view; `optionnel` is reserved for the
//              exercises past that, where the family already has an accessible
//              entry point. See exercisesByPattern() / coreExercises() at the bottom.
